---
import DocsFaq from "@/components/DocsFaq.astro"
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro"
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
import { docsMetadata } from "@/lib/docs-meta"
import { faqPageJsonLd } from "@/lib/faq"

const metadata = docsMetadata("/docs/install")


// Visible Q&A + matching FAQPage JSON-LD. Only methods that exist (go install,
// go get -tool + the verify/PATH/update/uninstall flow); no Homebrew/script/action.
const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How do I install the Gooze CLI?",
    a: "Run `go install gooze.dev/pkg/gooze@latest`, then make sure your Go bin directory (`$(go env GOPATH)/bin`) is on your `PATH`.",
  },
  {
    q: "What are the prerequisites for installing Gooze?",
    a: "Go 1.27.3 or newer and a Go module with a `go.mod` file. Gooze runs on Linux, macOS, and Windows.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I pin Gooze in `go.mod` and run it with `go tool`?",
    a: "Yes. Run `go get -tool gooze.dev/pkg/gooze@v1.4.1`, then `go tool gooze run ./...`. It raises your module's `go` line to `1.27.3` and adds about 55 indirect requirements to `go.mod`.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I verify Gooze is installed correctly?",
    a: "Run `gooze version`. It prints the gooze version and the Go version it was built with.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I fix `gooze: command not found` after installing?",
    a: "Your Go bin directory is not on your `PATH`. Add `$(go env GOPATH)/bin` to `PATH` (for example `export PATH=$PATH:$(go env GOPATH)/bin` in your shell profile) and restart the terminal.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I update Gooze to the latest version?",
    a: "Run `go install gooze.dev/pkg/gooze@latest` again. It fetches and builds the newest release over the old binary.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I uninstall Gooze?",
    a: "Delete the binary: `rm $(go env GOPATH)/bin/gooze`.",
  },
]


const faqJsonLd = faqPageJsonLd(faqs)
---
<DocsLayout metadata={metadata}>
    <section>
      <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />
      <h1 class="mt-0 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">Installation</h1>
      <p class="gz-prose gz-prose--lead mb-8">Gooze is one Go binary. Install it with <code>go install</code>, check that it runs, and
        you&apos;re ready for your first mutation run.</p>
      <div>
        <div id="requirements">
          <h2>What you need</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Go 1.27.3 or newer</strong> to build Gooze.</li>
            <li>A Go module with a <code>go.mod</code> file.</li>
            <li>Linux, macOS, or Windows.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>If you see install or build errors, update Go first.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="install">
          <h2>Install Gooze</h2>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="go install gooze.dev/pkg/gooze@latest" />
          <p>Go downloads and builds Gooze and puts the <code>gooze</code> binary in your Go bin
            directory.</p>
          <p>Gooze needs Go 1.27.3 to build. On an older Go, let Go fetch the toolchain
            for the install; your project keeps its own Go version:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="GOTOOLCHAIN=auto go install gooze.dev/pkg/gooze@latest" />
        </div>
        <div id="go-tool">
          <h2>Pin Gooze in go.mod (go tool)</h2>
          <p>You want everyone on the team, and CI, to run the same Gooze version without a
            separate install step. Add it to your module as a tool (this needs Go 1.24 or newer):</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="go get -tool gooze.dev/pkg/gooze@v1.4.1" />
          <p>Then run it through <code>go tool</code>; Go builds it on first use:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="go tool gooze run ./..." />
          <p>This changes your <code>go.mod</code>:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>It adds a <code>tool gooze.dev/pkg/gooze</code> line.</li>
            <li>It raises the module&apos;s <code>go</code> line to <code>1.27.3</code>, the Go
              version Gooze needs, so every build of your module needs it too. With{" "}<code>GOTOOLCHAIN=auto</code>, an older Go downloads that toolchain.</li>
            <li>It adds Gooze&apos;s dependencies as indirect requirements, about 55 of them for{" "}<code>v1.4.1</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>If your module must stay on an older Go, or you don&apos;t want the extra requirements,
            use <code>go install</code> above instead. To upgrade, run <code>go get -tool</code>{" "}again with the new version.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="verify">
          <h2>Verify the install</h2>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze version" />
          <p>It prints the gooze version and the Go version it was built with. Include this output
            when you report a bug.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="path">
          <h2>If you get &quot;command not found&quot;</h2>
          <p>If your shell answers <code>gooze: command not found</code>, the Go bin directory
            (usually <code>$(go env GOPATH)/bin</code>) isn&apos;t on your <code>PATH</code>.
            Print it and add it to <code>PATH</code>:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code='echo "$(go env GOPATH)/bin"' />
        </div>
        <div id="tty">
          <h2>Interactive vs CI output</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>In a terminal (TTY), Gooze shows an interactive UI.</li>
            <li>In CI (or when output is redirected), Gooze prints plain text.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div id="logs">
          <h2>Logs</h2>
          <p>If a run looks wrong, run it again with <code>--verbose</code>.
            Then check the log file (<code>.gooze.log</code> by default; set another path
            with <code>--log-output</code>).</p>
          <div>
            <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze run --verbose ./..." />
          </div>
        </div>
        <DocsFaq faqs={faqs} />
        <p>Gooze is installed. Next, run it on a module and find the tests you&apos;re missing in
          the <a href="/docs/quick-start/">Quick start</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
</DocsLayout>
